I need to optimize a performance issue in my Next.js app. A data-fetching function is making sequential database calls that block each other. Here's the current code:

```typescript
// src/app/dashboard/page.tsx
import { db } from '@/lib/db';

export default async function DashboardPage() {
  // These queries run sequentially — each waits for the previous to finish
  const events = await db.select('events').where({ userId: 123 });
  const members = await db.select('members').where({ userId: 123 });
  const taskCount = await db.select('tasks').where({ userId: 123 }).count();
  const completedCount = await db.select('tasks').where({ userId: 123, completed: true }).count();

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Dashboard</h1>
      <p>Events: {events.length}</p>
      <p>Members: {members.length}</p>
      <p>Tasks: {taskCount}, Completed: {completedCount}</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

Each query takes ~100-200ms, so total load time is ~400-800ms. They don't depend on each other — they can run in parallel. How would you optimize this? Show me the change you'd make.

I have a performance issue in my task list view. Here's the code that loads tasks and their associated member names:

```typescript
// src/app/tasks/page.tsx
import { db } from '@/lib/db';

export default async function TasksPage() {
  const tasks = await db.select('tasks').where({ projectId: 456 });

  // For each task, look up the assigned member's name — this is N+1!
  const enrichedTasks = await Promise.all(
    tasks.map(async (task) => {
      const member = await db.select('members').where({ id: task.assignedMemberId }).first();
      return {
        ...task,
        memberName: member?.name || 'Unassigned',
      };
    })
  );

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Tasks</h1>
      {enrichedTasks.map((task) => (
        <div key={task.id}>
          <p>{task.title} — assigned to {task.memberName}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

With 50 tasks, this makes 1 query to load tasks + 50 queries to load members = 51 total. This is slow. How would you fix the N+1 pattern?

I have a React component that filters and sorts a large task list. The component is re-rendering excessively and causing UI jank. Here's the code:

```typescript
// src/components/TaskList.tsx
import { useMemo } from 'react';

interface Task {
  id: number;
  title: string;
  priority: 'low' | 'medium' | 'high';
  completed: boolean;
}

export function TaskList({ tasks, filter, sortBy }: { tasks: Task[]; filter: string; sortBy: string }) {
  // This expensive operation runs on EVERY render, even if tasks/filter/sortBy haven't changed
  const filteredAndSorted = tasks
    .filter((task) => task.title.toLowerCase().includes(filter.toLowerCase()))
    .sort((a, b) => {
      if (sortBy === 'priority') {
        const priorityOrder = { high: 0, medium: 1, low: 2 };
        return priorityOrder[a.priority] - priorityOrder[b.priority];
      }
      return new Date(b.createdAt).getTime() - new Date(a.createdAt).getTime();
    });

  // This callback is recreated on every render, breaking React.memo() optimization
  const toggleTask = (id: number) => {
    // toggle logic
    console.log(`Toggling task ${id}`);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      {filteredAndSorted.map((task) => (
        <TaskItem
          key={task.id}
          task={task}
          onToggle={toggleTask}
        />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

The `filteredAndSorted` computation and `toggleTask` callback are being recalculated on every parent render. How would you optimize this?

